• Without , the code runs in 11.54 seconds.• Without std::sort(data, data + arraySize);std::sort(data, data + arraySize);, the code runs in 11.54 seconds.• With the sorted data, the code runs in 1.93 seconds.• With the sorted data, the code runs in 1.93 seconds.(Sorting itself takes more time than this one pass over the array, so it's not actually worth doing if we needed to calculate this for an unknown array.)(Sorting itself takes more time than this one pass over the array, so it's not actually worth doing if we needed to calculate this for an unknown array.)Initially, I thought this might be just a language or compiler anomaly, so I tried Java:Initially, I thought this might be just a language or compiler anomaly, so I tried Java:

Copyimport java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Random;

public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        // Generate data
        int arraySize = 32768;
        int data[] = new int[arraySize];

        Random rnd = new Random(0);
        for (int c = 0; c < arraySize; ++c)
            data[c] = rnd.nextInt() % 256;

        // !!! With this, the next loop runs faster
        Arrays.sort(data);

        // Test
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        long sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; ++i)
        {
            for (int c = 0; c < arraySize; ++c)
            {   // Primary loop.
                if (data[c] >= 128)
                    sum += data[c];
            }
        }

        System.out.println((System.nanoTime() - start) / 1000000000.0);
        System.out.println("sum = " + sum);
    }
}
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Random;

public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        // Generate data
        int arraySize = 32768;
        int data[] = new int[arraySize];

        Random rnd = new Random(0);
        for (int c = 0; c < arraySize; ++c)
            data[c] = rnd.nextInt() % 256;

        // !!! With this, the next loop runs faster
        Arrays.sort(data);

        // Test
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        long sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; ++i)
        {
            for (int c = 0; c < arraySize; ++c)
            {   // Primary loop.
                if (data[c] >= 128)
                    sum += data[c];
            }
        }

        System.out.println((System.nanoTime() - start) / 1000000000.0);
        System.out.println("sum = " + sum);
    }
}
With a similar but less extreme result.With a similar but less extreme result.My first thought was that sorting brings the data into the , but that's silly because the array was just generated.My first thought was that sorting brings the data into the cachecache, but that's silly because the array was just generated.• What is going on?• What is going on?• Why is processing a sorted array faster than processing an unsorted array?• Why is processing a sorted array faster than processing an unsorted array?The code is summing up some independent terms, so the order should not matter.The code is summing up some independent terms, so the order should not matter.Related / follow-up Q&As with more modern C++ compilersRelated / follow-up Q&As with more modern C++ compilers•  - , especially when SSE4.1 or AVX2 is available. This avoids any data-dependent branching so performance isn't data-dependent.• Why is processing an unsorted array the same speed as processing a sorted array with modern x86-64 clang?Why is processing an unsorted array the same speed as processing a sorted array with modern x86-64 clang? - modern C++ compilers auto-vectorize the loopmodern C++ compilers auto-vectorize the loop, especially when SSE4.1 or AVX2 is available. This avoids any data-dependent branching so performance isn't data-dependent.•  - branchless scalar with  can result in a longer dependency chain (especially when GCC chooses poorly), creating a latency bottleneck that makes it slower than branchy asm for the sorted case.• gcc optimization flag -O3 makes code slower than -O2gcc optimization flag -O3 makes code slower than -O2 - branchless scalar with cmovcmov can result in a longer dependency chain (especially when GCC chooses poorly), creating a latency bottleneck that makes it slower than branchy asm for the sorted case.
