@screwnut: To be fair, though, it still wouldn't be worth partitioning first, because partitioning requires conditional copying or swapping based on the same  compare. (Unless you're going to be counting multiple times, and you want to partition the bulk of an array so it's still fast, only mispredicting in an unpartitioned part after some appends or modifications). If you can get the compiler to do it, ideally just vectorize with SIMD, or at least use branchless scalar if the data is unpredictable. (See previous comment for links.)@screwnut: To be fair, though, it still wouldn't be worth partitioning first, because partitioning requires conditional copying or swapping based on the same array[i] > 128array[i] > 128 compare. (Unless you're going to be counting multiple times, and you want to partition the bulk of an array so it's still fast, only mispredicting in an unpartitioned part after some appends or modifications). If you can get the compiler to do it, ideally just vectorize with SIMD, or at least use branchless scalar if the data is unpredictable. (See previous comment for links.)
