This is delightful, but for anyone who might not understand the joke: the purpose of the  in the original code is to run the code being benchmarked many times so as to be able to more accurately measure the time taken to execute it—measurements that would otherwise be compromised by limited timer resolution and/or jitter caused by other processes (including the OS) unpredictably preempting this one. It is true that the benchmarked code does take much, much less time to execute if you run it only once!This is delightful, but for anyone who might not understand the joke: the purpose of the for (unsigned i = 0; i < 100000; ++i)for (unsigned i = 0; i < 100000; ++i) in the original code is to run the code being benchmarked many times so as to be able to more accurately measure the time taken to execute it—measurements that would otherwise be compromised by limited timer resolution and/or jitter caused by other processes (including the OS) unpredictably preempting this one. It is true that the benchmarked code does take much, much less time to execute if you run it only once!35Reply









