I don't really see how this proves anything? The only thing you have shown is that "not doing all the work of sorting the whole array takes less time than sorting the whole array". Your claim that this "also runs fastest" is very architecture-dependent. See my answer about how this works on ARM. PS you could make your code faster on non-ARM architectures by putting the summation inside the 200-element block loop, sorting in reverse, and then using Yochai Timmer's suggestion of breaking once you get an out-of range value. That way each 200-element block summation can be terminated early.I don't really see how this proves anything? The only thing you have shown is that "not doing all the work of sorting the whole array takes less time than sorting the whole array". Your claim that this "also runs fastest" is very architecture-dependent. See my answer about how this works on ARM. PS you could make your code faster on non-ARM architectures by putting the summation inside the 200-element block loop, sorting in reverse, and then using Yochai Timmer's suggestion of breaking once you get an out-of range value. That way each 200-element block summation can be terminated early.9Reply









